# =============================================================================
# inf — no tensor element
# =============================================================================
#
# What this teaches:
#   • A tensor element must have a device layout; `inf` is a heap value on
#     the host, so `tensor<inf, [2]>` is rejected where the type is written
#     (`tensor_element_unbounded`), not later at a use.
#   • `lista<inf>` is the host collection of unbounded integers.
#
# Common mistakes:
#   • reaching for a tensor to hold big integers — a tensor is for device
#     work on fixed-width numbers.
#
# See also: inf, tensor, lista
# =============================================================================

# typi/inf-tensor-error — `inf` as a tensor element
#
# WHY: pins F9 rulings 39(a) and 47: the rejection is the same on every
# machine, at the declaration.
#
# EXPECTED OUTPUT:
#   inf-tensor-error.expected — the stable diagnostic identity.

fn shaped(tensor<inf, [2]> values) → void {
}

main {
    print 1
}
